description:
event.rs:854-863 — when CalendarEvent/query is called with 'expandRecurrences: true', the handler enforces the §5.11 'filter MUST be a single FilterCondition with both before and after' rule BEFORE invoking the backend:

  if expand_recurrences {
      let bounds_ok = filter.as_ref()
          .is_some_and(|f| f.before.is_some() && f.after.is_some());
      if !bounds_ok {
          return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(
              'expandRecurrences requires a FilterCondition with both before and after',
          ));
      }
  }

Without this guard the backend would have to enumerate an unbounded set of recurrence instances — the spec says the server MUST enforce this bound. Pushing it into the handler:
1. Saves a backend round-trip for malformed requests.
2. Centralizes the error reporting (one error string, not N variants per backend).
3. Means a buggy backend that DOES try to enumerate cannot get the chance.

A future contributor might suggest 'let backends decide what 'bounded' means' — no. The spec is normative, the handler is the right enforcement point because it pre-dates any backend behavior.

DEFEND when reviewers suggest pushing this into the backend.
